<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-jacobian-substitution" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Substitution in Multiple Integrals and the Jacobian Determinant</title>

<introduction>
<p>
In single-variable calculus, substitution converts a difficult integral
into an easier one at the cost of a correction factor <m>dx/du</m>. In
this section we develop the analogous tool for double and triple
integrals. The correction factor is now a determinant<mdash/>the
<term>Jacobian determinant</term><mdash/>which measures how a change of
variables stretches or shrinks area and volume.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-jacobian-determinant">
<title>The Jacobian Determinant</title>

<p>
Assume that there exists a transformation <m>T</m> that relates two
coordinate systems, <m>(u,v)</m> and <m>(x,y)</m> coordinates. In other
words, there exist two functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> such that
<m>x = f(u,v)</m> and <m>y = g(u,v)</m>. As illustrated in
<xref ref="fig-transformation-blobs"/>, the region <m>S</m> in the
<m>uv</m>-plane is transformed to the region <m>R</m> in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, and the inverse transformation <m>T^{-1}</m> carries
<m>R</m> back to <m>S</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-transformation-blobs">
<caption>
The transformation <m>T(u,v) = (f(u,v), g(u,v))</m> carries the region
<m>S</m> in the <m>uv</m>-plane onto the region <m>R</m> in the
<m>xy</m>-plane; the inverse <m>T^{-1}</m> carries <m>R</m> back onto
<m>S</m>. The point <m>(u_1, v_1)</m> is sent to the point
<m>(x_1, y_1)</m>.
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="46% 46%" valign="middle">
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-uvplane-blob">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="10">
<definition>blobS(t) = (1.55 + (1 + 0.22*sin(2*t))*cos(t), 1.7 + (0.78 + 0.1*cos(3*t))*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.5,3.5,3.5)">
<axes at="uv-axes" xlabel="u" ylabel="v" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="uv-blob" function="blobS" domain="(0,2*pi)"
                  closed="yes" fill="#f6c78e" stroke="#c07020"
                  thickness="2"/>
<point at="uv-point" p="(1.3,1.45)" fill="black" size="3"
       alignment="se">
<m>(u_1,v_1)</m>
</point>
<label at="uv-S" anchor="(1.05,2.0)" alignment="c">
<m>S</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blob-shaped region S, shaded orange, in the u v plane. A point labeled u 1 comma v 1 is marked inside the region.">
  <annotation ref="uv-blob" text="The region S in the u v plane, the domain of the transformation T."/>
  <annotation ref="uv-point" text="A sample point u 1 comma v 1 inside S."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-xyplane-blob">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="10">
<definition>blobR(t) = (1.75 + (1.05 + 0.18*cos(2*t))*cos(t), 1.6 + (0.72 + 0.14*sin(3*t))*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.5,3.5,3.5)">
<axes at="xy-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="xy-blob" function="blobR" domain="(0,2*pi)"
                  closed="yes" fill="#9ecfff" stroke="#2b6cb0"
                  thickness="2"/>
<point at="xy-point" p="(2.2,1.4)" fill="black" size="3"
       alignment="se">
<m>(x_1,y_1)</m>
</point>
<label at="xy-R" anchor="(1.35,1.95)" alignment="c">
<m>R</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blob-shaped region R, shaded blue, in the x y plane. A point labeled x 1 comma y 1 is marked inside the region. It is the image of the region S under the transformation T.">
  <annotation ref="xy-blob" text="The region R in the x y plane, the image of S under the transformation T."/>
  <annotation ref="xy-point" text="The image point x 1 comma y 1, corresponding to u 1 comma v 1."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

<definition xml:id="def-jacobian">
<statement>
<p>
The <term>Jacobian determinant</term> of the transformation
<m>x = f(u,v)</m>, <m>y = g(u,v)</m> is defined as
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-jacobian-2d">
J(u,v) = \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
= \begin{vmatrix}
\dfrac{\partial x}{\partial u} \amp \dfrac{\partial x}{\partial v} \\[2ex]
\dfrac{\partial y}{\partial u} \amp \dfrac{\partial y}{\partial v}
\end{vmatrix}
= \frac{\partial x}{\partial u}\frac{\partial y}{\partial v}
- \frac{\partial x}{\partial v}\frac{\partial y}{\partial u}.
</men>

<p>
In 3D, this definition is generalized to
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-jacobian-3d">
J(u,v,w) = \frac{\partial(x,y,z)}{\partial(u,v,w)}
= \begin{vmatrix}
\dfrac{\partial x}{\partial u} \amp \dfrac{\partial x}{\partial v} \amp \dfrac{\partial x}{\partial w} \\[2ex]
\dfrac{\partial y}{\partial u} \amp \dfrac{\partial y}{\partial v} \amp \dfrac{\partial y}{\partial w} \\[2ex]
\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial u} \amp \dfrac{\partial z}{\partial v} \amp \dfrac{\partial z}{\partial w}
\end{vmatrix}.
</men>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
Then the multiple integrals can be converted from one coordinate system
to another as follows:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-sub-2d">
\iint_R F(x,y)\, dx\, dy
= \iint_S F\big(f(u,v),\, g(u,v)\big)
\left| \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)} \right| du\, dv,
</men>

<p>
and in three dimensions, with <m>x = f(u,v,w)</m>, <m>y = g(u,v,w)</m>,
<m>z = h(u,v,w)</m> carrying a region <m>E</m> in <m>uvw</m>-space onto
a region <m>D</m> in <m>xyz</m>-space,
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-sub-3d">
\iiint_D F(x,y,z)\, dx\, dy\, dz
= \iiint_E F\big(f(u,v,w),\, g(u,v,w),\, h(u,v,w)\big)
\left| \frac{\partial(x,y,z)}{\partial(u,v,w)} \right| du\, dv\, dw.
</men>

<p>
The above integrals should be compared to the single-variable
substitution <m>x = h(u)</m>:
</p>

<me>
\int_{x=h(a)}^{x=h(b)} f(x)\, dx = \int_{u=a}^{u=b} f(h(u))\, \frac{dx}{du}\, du.
</me>

<p>
Note that in the past we used to use the substitution of the form
<m>u = g(x)</m> and write:
</p>

<me>
\int_{x=a}^{x=b} f(g(x))\, \frac{du}{dx}\, dx = \int_{u=g(a)}^{u=g(b)} f(u)\, du.
</me>

<p>
The derivative factor <m>dx/du</m> records how much the substitution
stretches lengths. In two variables, the Jacobian determinant plays the
same role for areas: <m>|J|</m> is the local ratio of area in the
<m>xy</m>-plane to area in the <m>uv</m>-plane. The next example makes
this ratio concrete.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-jacobian-rectangle">
<title>A First Substitution: Scaling a Rectangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use the transformation <m>u = 3x</m>, <m>v = 2y</m> to evaluate
</p>

<me>
\int_{y=0}^{1} \int_{x=0}^{1} 6xy\, dx\, dy,
</me>

<p>
and verify that the Jacobian determinant equals the ratio of the areas
of the two regions of integration.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-jacobian-rectangle">
<p>
The transformation is <m>T(u,v) = (x,y) = \left( \tfrac{u}{3},
\tfrac{v}{2} \right)</m>, which carries the rectangle
<m>S = [0,3] \times [0,2]</m> in the <m>uv</m>-plane onto the unit
square <m>R = [0,1] \times [0,1]</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, as shown
in <xref ref="fig-jacobian-rectangle"/>. By
<xref ref="eq-jacobian-2d"/>, the Jacobian determinant is
</p>

<me>
\frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
= \begin{vmatrix}
\frac{1}{3} \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \frac{1}{2}
\end{vmatrix}
= \frac{1}{6},
</me>

<p>
which is exactly the ratio of the areas,
<m>\dfrac{A_R}{A_S} = \dfrac{1}{6}</m>: the map shrinks areas by the
factor <m>6</m>, uniformly. Substituting <m>x = u/3</m>,
<m>y = v/2</m>, and <m>dx\, dy = \tfrac{1}{6}\, du\, dv</m>,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\int_0^1 \int_0^1 6xy\, dx\, dy
\amp= \int_{v=0}^{2} \int_{u=0}^{3} 6 \left( \frac{u}{3} \right)
\left( \frac{v}{2} \right) \left( \frac{1}{3} \right)
\left( \frac{1}{2} \right) du\, dv</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{6} \int_0^2 \int_0^3 uv\, du\, dv
= \frac{1}{6} \left( \frac{9}{2} \right) (2) = \frac{3}{2},</mrow>
</md>

<p>
which agrees with computing the integral directly:
<m>\int_0^1 \int_0^1 6xy\, dx\, dy = 6 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} \cdot
\tfrac{1}{2} = \tfrac{3}{2}</m>. The animation in
<xref ref="fig-jacobian-rectangle-video"/> shows how the Jacobian acts
as an area conversion factor.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-jacobian-rectangle-video">
<caption>
The transformation <m>T(u,v) = (u/3, v/2)</m> maps the rectangle
<m>S = [0,3] \times [0,2]</m> onto the unit square <m>R</m>. The
Jacobian <m>1/6</m> is the ratio of the areas.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-jacobian-rectangle"
       source="videos/jacobian_area_scale_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-jacobian-rectangle">
<caption>
The rectangle <m>S = [0,3] \times [0,2]</m> in the <m>uv</m>-plane
(right) and its image, the unit square <m>R</m>, in the
<m>xy</m>-plane (left) under <m>T(u,v) = (u/3, v/2)</m>. The area ratio
<m>A_R / A_S = 1/6</m> equals the Jacobian determinant.
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="46% 46%" valign="middle">
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rect-R">
<diagram dimensions="(280,280)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.5,3.6,2.9)">
<grid-axes at="rr-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="rr-square" points="((0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(0,1))"
         fill="#9ecfff" stroke="#2b6cb0" thickness="2"/>
<label at="rr-R" anchor="(0.5,0.5)" alignment="c">
<m>R</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The unit square R, shaded blue, with corners at the origin and 1 comma 1 in the x y plane.">
  <annotation ref="rr-square" text="The unit square R, the image of the rectangle S under the transformation."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rect-S">
<diagram dimensions="(280,280)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.5,3.6,2.9)">
<grid-axes at="rs-grid" xlabel="u" ylabel="v"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="rs-rect" points="((0,0),(3,0),(3,2),(0,2))"
         fill="#f6c78e" stroke="#c07020" thickness="2"/>
<label at="rs-S" anchor="(1.5,1)" alignment="c">
<m>S</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
