<annotation ref="figure" text="The rectangle S, shaded orange, with corners at the origin and 3 comma 2 in the u v plane. Its area is 6 times the area of the unit square R.">
  <annotation ref="rs-rect" text="The rectangle S from 0 to 3 in u and 0 to 2 in v. The transformation shrinks it onto the unit square."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-substitution-examples">
<title>Choosing a Transformation</title>

<p>
In practice we usually run the process in the other direction: the
region of integration <m>R</m> is awkward in <m>xy</m>-coordinates, and
we look for a substitution under which it corresponds to a simple
region<mdash/>ideally a rectangle<mdash/>with constant limits of
integration. The boundary curves of <m>R</m> usually tell us what
<m>u</m> and <m>v</m> should be.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-parallelogram-substitution">
<title>A Parallelogram Region</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use the transformation <m>T(u,v) = (u - 2v,\, v)</m> to compute
</p>

<me>
\iint_D (x + 3y)\, dx\, dy,
</me>

<p>
where <m>D</m> is the parallelogram bounded by the lines
<m>x + 2y = 6</m>, <m>x + 2y = 10</m>, <m>y = 1</m>, and <m>y = 3</m>,
shown in <xref ref="fig-parallelogram-sub"/>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-parallelogram-substitution">
<p>
The transformation gives <m>x = u - 2v</m> and <m>y = v</m>, so by
<xref ref="eq-jacobian-2d"/>,
</p>

<me>
\frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
= \begin{vmatrix}
1 \amp -2 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{vmatrix}
= 1.
</me>

<p>
Inverting the transformation, <m>u = x + 2y</m> and <m>v = y</m>.
Therefore the boundary lines <m>x + 2y = 6</m> and <m>x + 2y = 10</m>
become the vertical lines <m>u = 6</m> and <m>u = 10</m>, while
<m>y = 1</m> and <m>y = 3</m> become <m>v = 1</m> and <m>v = 3</m>:
the parallelogram <m>D</m> corresponds to the rectangle
</p>

<me>
R = \{ (u,v) : 6 \le u \le 10,\ 1 \le v \le 3 \}.
</me>

<p>
The integrand becomes
<m>x + 3y = (u - 2v) + 3v = u + v</m>, so by
<xref ref="eq-sub-2d"/>,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\iint_D (x + 3y)\, dx\, dy
\amp= \int_1^3 \int_6^{10} (u + v)\, \lvert 1 \rvert\, du\, dv
= \int_1^3 \left[ \frac{u^2}{2} + uv \right]_{u=6}^{u=10} dv</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \int_1^3 (32 + 4v)\, dv
= \Big[ 32v + 2v^2 \Big]_1^3
= 64 + 16 = 80.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Watch the region unfold into a rectangle in
<xref ref="fig-parallelogram-sub-video"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-parallelogram-sub-video">
<caption>
The substitution <m>u = x + 2y</m>, <m>v = y</m> straightens the
parallelogram <m>D</m> into the rectangle
<m>[6,10] \times [1,3]</m>; the Jacobian equals <m>1</m>, so areas are
preserved.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-parallelogram-sub"
       source="videos/parallelogram_sub_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-parallelogram-sub">
<caption>
The parallelogram <m>D</m> between the lines <m>x + 2y = 6</m>,
<m>x + 2y = 10</m>, <m>y = 1</m>, and <m>y = 3</m> (left), and the
corresponding rectangle <m>R = [6,10] \times [1,3]</m> in the
<m>uv</m>-plane (right).
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="48% 44%" valign="middle">
<image>
