<example xml:id="example-curved-to-rectangle">
<title>A Curved Region Mapped to a Rectangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>D</m> be the region in the first quadrant bounded by the
hyperbolas <m>x^2 - y^2 = -3</m>, <m>x^2 - y^2 = 3</m>, <m>xy = 1</m>,
and <m>xy = 4</m>, shown in <xref ref="fig-curved-region"/>.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find a transformation <m>T</m> that relates <m>D</m> to a rectangle
<m>R</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute the integral <m>\displaystyle \iint_D xy\, (x^2 + y^2)\, dx\, dy</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-curved-to-rectangle">
<p>
<em>Part A.</em> The boundary curves suggest the substitution
</p>

<me>
u = x^2 - y^2, \qquad v = xy,
</me>

<p>
since then the four boundary curves become the four lines
<m>u = -3</m>, <m>u = 3</m>, <m>v = 1</m>, and <m>v = 4</m>. Under this
substitution the curved region <m>D</m> corresponds to the rectangle
</p>

<me>
R = \{ (u,v) : -3 \le u \le 3,\ 1 \le v \le 4 \}.
</me>

<p>
<em>Part B.</em> Here the substitution is given in the direction
<m>u = u(x,y)</m>, <m>v = v(x,y)</m>, so it is easier to first compute
the Jacobian of <m>(u,v)</m> with respect to <m>(x,y)</m>:
</p>

<me>
\frac{\partial(u,v)}{\partial(x,y)}
= \begin{vmatrix}
2x \amp -2y \\
y \amp x
\end{vmatrix}
= 2x^2 + 2y^2 = 2(x^2 + y^2).
</me>

<p>
According to the inverse function theorem, the Jacobian we need is the
reciprocal of this one:
</p>

<me>
\frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
= \left( \frac{\partial(u,v)}{\partial(x,y)} \right)^{-1}
= \frac{1}{2(x^2 + y^2)}.
</me>

<p>
Now apply <xref ref="eq-sub-2d"/>. The factor <m>x^2 + y^2</m> in the
integrand cancels against the Jacobian, and <m>xy = v</m>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\iint_D xy\,(x^2 + y^2)\, dx\, dy
\amp= \iint_R v\, (x^2 + y^2) \cdot \frac{1}{2(x^2 + y^2)}\, du\, dv</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2} \int_{-3}^{3} \int_1^4 v\, dv\, du
= \frac{1}{2}\, (6) \left( \frac{15}{2} \right)
= \frac{45}{2}.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Without the substitution, even describing <m>D</m> with explicit
<m>xy</m>-limits would be painful; with it, the integral collapses in
two lines. See <xref ref="fig-curved-region-video"/> for the animation.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-curved-region-video">
<caption>
The substitution <m>u = x^2 - y^2</m>, <m>v = xy</m> maps the region
between the four hyperbolas onto the rectangle
<m>[-3,3] \times [1,4]</m>, and the Jacobian cancels the factor
<m>x^2 + y^2</m> in the integrand.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-curved-region-sub"
       source="videos/curved_region_sub_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-curved-region">
<caption>
The region <m>D</m> bounded by the hyperbolas <m>x^2 - y^2 = \pm 3</m>
and <m>xy = 1</m>, <m>xy = 4</m> (left) corresponds to the rectangle
<m>R = [-3,3] \times [1,4]</m> under <m>u = x^2 - y^2</m>,
<m>v = xy</m> (right).
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="46% 46%" valign="middle">
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-curved-D">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="10">
<definition>hyp(x,y) = x^2 - y^2</definition>
<definition>prod(x,y) = x*y</definition>
